# Deploying changes to the live app

The running app at `http://<host>:3000` is the systemd unit
`se-deal-workspace.service` running **`next start` in production mode**. It
serves the prebuilt `.next` directory and does **NOT** hot-reload from source.

**Merging to `main` updates git only — it does NOT update the running app.**
A feature is not "live" until the production bundle is rebuilt and the service
is restarted.

## Workflow for every feature

1. Branch (`feat/...` or `fix/...`) -> commit -> push -> merge into `main` (`--no-ff`) -> push `main`.
2. **Deploy:** from the repo root run `npm run deploy` (or `bash deploy.sh`).
3. Tell the user to hard-refresh (`Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R`).

## Why `deploy.sh` works without sudo

The unit runs as this user with `Restart=always`, so the script restarts it by
killing its main process and letting systemd respawn it — no sudo needed.
`deploy.sh` builds first, then restarts, then verifies a CSS asset returns 200.

## Do NOT

- Do NOT run `npm run build` by itself against the live dir without an immediate
  restart. Rebuilding under a running `next start` invalidates the hashed
  CSS/JS chunks the live server references, which 404s them and renders the app
  unstyled. Always build + restart together (i.e. use `deploy.sh`).
- Do NOT start a stray `npm run dev`; it cannot own port 3000 (the service holds
  it) and only causes confusion.
